%% ============================================================
%% F1: All dt>1 degrade
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\%$ of baseline); fixed $\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\%$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Precedes Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison---008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON)---differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 0.97\times$ (eval 503.0). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 27 of 30 experimental configurations across two environments (AUROC${=}0.953$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated, Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{NVE trajectories (mean $\pm$ seed range). Trained $\dt$-embedding (red, Walker Walk, $n{=}3$ seeds plotted; seed~4 data in Table~\ref{tab:complete_results}) exceeds the $1.5\times$ threshold (dashed) in all seeds; no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\times$. Three trajectory types emerge: early collapse (seed~0), late-onset collapse (seed~3), and self-correction (seed~2). Dotted line marks the 35K diagnostic window onset.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trained $\dt$-embedding precedes CIVO in all five Walker Walk seeds (peak NVE: $4.72\times$, $4.62\times$, $4.12\times$, $1.62\times$, $1.53\times$), universally degrading performance to below 30\% of baseline (IQM $175.2$, mean $170.6$, $n{=}5$; \citealt{agarwal2021precipice}). Downstream trajectories diverge: two seeds undergo full collapse, one exhibits late-onset collapse still declining at 105K, and one self-corrects (seed 2); seed 5 exhibits NVE self-correction ($4.72\times \to 1.22\times$) but policy remains collapsed (eval 10.5\% of baseline). Combined with Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset is universal across all seven tested seeds ($n{=}7$, two environments) with 6/7 suffering sustained policy collapse within the observation window, 1/7 self-correcting (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly sufficient in tested configurations} for CIVO. Neither factor alone precedes pathological overestimation: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, 3 seeds, peak NVE $0.83$--$0.93\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, 3 seeds, peak NVE $\leq 1.41\times$) both remain below $1.5\times$. Cross-environment replication on Cheetah Run confirms generalization. Freezing the embedding (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}) dramatically reduces severity (NVE $0.19$--$1.88\times$ vs.\ trained $1.53$--$4.72\times$; 2/4 seeds below threshold, 2/4 transient self-correcting spikes; Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}); removing the embedding (008f) also avoids CIVO. Partial gradient blocking (exp\_044) does not prevent onset (peak NVE $4.45\times$) but permits delayed self-correction (final eval 538, 59\% baseline; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}), confirming that \emph{any} gradient pathway updating $e_{\dt}$ suffices to induce CIVO. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}).

\paragraph{ManiSkill manipulation tasks.}
None of three ManiSkill environments exhibit $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO. StackCube-v1 value-ratio spikes are environment-specific: trained (049/052, NVE $5.05$/$4.67\times$) and frozen (051, $4.30\times$) match, all self-correcting by 82.5K. PegInsertionSide-v1 exhibits high-amplitude stochastic dynamics ($4$--$11\times$) whose trained/frozen ordering is seed-dependent: seed~0 trained peaks higher ($10.05\times$ vs.\ frozen $1.82\times$), but seed~1 reverses (frozen peak $11.32\times$ vs.\ trained peak $3.08\times$). PickCube-v1 frozen control (exp\_054, peak $8.81\times$ at 62.5K, self-correcting) confirms the same pattern. CIVO is locomotion-specific in DreamerV3; the frozen-embedding diagnostic correctly identifies these transients as non-CIVO.

%% ============================================================
%% F6--F7: Interventions and cross-environment
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions and Multiple Pathways}
\label{sec:f6}

\paragraph{F6: Trained embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways.}
Exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Two of four seeds collapse (50\%): Pathway~A (CIVO, $\nve = 4.02\times$) and preliminary Pathway~B (entropy \emph{increase}, $\nve = 3.78\times$; 2 seeds only, requires confirmation; Appendix~\ref{sec:pathways}). Pathway~B produces one of three non-CIVO false positives ($n{=}30$).
\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Full-SGS (exp\_018) detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$. Seed~1 shows partial mitigation (eval 448.3, NVE $1.66\times$); seed~2 collapses (0/2 elimination; Appendix~\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah (010b, NVE $8.27$--$108.52\times$) confirms CIVO generalization; full-SGS yields 0/3 elimination. Removing the embedding eliminates CIVO on Cheetah (024b, NVE $< 0.2\times$).

\paragraph{Targeted obs-decoder-only SGS confirms pathway redundancy.}
Obs-decoder-only SGS (exp\_025/026) achieves 1/6 CIVO prevention (1/4 Walker, 0/2 Cheetah; Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}), not meaningfully different from full-pathway SGS (0/3). CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels: blocking any single pathway shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

